<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Constitution of India</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1950">1950</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Constitution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> is the supreme law of the Republic of India, adopted by the Constituent Assembly on 26 November 1949 and in force from 26 January 1950, a date celebrated as Republic Day. Drafted by a committee chaired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a>, it is the longest written national constitution in the world and establishes a federal parliamentary republic with guaranteed fundamental rights.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constitution-of-india.jpg" alt="Constitution of India" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Illumination/ornamentation by Beohar Rammanohar Sinha, calligraphy by Prem Behari Narain Raizada. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Constitution_of_India.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1928 the All Parties Conference convened a committee at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a> to prepare a constitution for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, whose report became known as the Nehru Report. The constitution that was eventually adopted drew mainly on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india-act-1935">Government of India Act 1935</a>, which was largely drafted by Samuel Hoare. Its framers also borrowed features of earlier legislation: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india-act-1858">Government of India Act 1858</a>, the Indian Councils Acts of 1861, 1892 and 1909, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india-act-1919">Government of India Act 1919</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence-act-1947">Indian Independence Act 1947</a>. The last of these, which led to the creation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, divided the former Constituent Assembly in two, and each new assembly had sovereign power to draft a constitution for its own state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The Constituent Assembly was elected by the members of the provincial assemblies. It first met on 9 December 1946, and on 11 December appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-prasad">Rajendra Prasad</a> as its president and B. N. Rau as constitutional legal adviser. Its 389 members were reduced to 299 after partition. On 13 December 1946 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a> presented an Objective Resolution laying down the underlying principles, which later became the Preamble. The assembly took almost three years to draft the constitution, holding eleven sessions over 165 days. Rau's initial draft had 243 articles and 13 schedules; after discussion, debate and amendment the text came to 395 articles and 8 schedules, in 22 parts. A seven-member drafting committee chaired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a> considered and amended Rau's draft.</p><p>The constitution declares <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> a sovereign, socialist, secular and democratic republic, though the words socialist and secular were added to the Preamble by the 42nd amendment in 1976. It sets up a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary system</a> in which the Council of Ministers, headed by the prime minister, is collectively responsible to the lower house, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lok-sabha">Lok Sabha</a>. Its first article describes India as a Union of States. The states retain important powers and are represented in the Rajya Sabha, but the Union List gives the national government exclusive authority over matters such as defence and international relations. Article 368 sets the procedure for amendment, which needs a two-thirds majority in each house of Parliament.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The framers repealed earlier British acts in Article 395, to ensure constitutional autochthony. The text is deliberately worded in generalities, to ensure flexibility. The American historian Granville Austin called it first and foremost a social document, with its Fundamental Rights and Directive Principles as its chief instruments and conscience. It does not limit Parliament's power of amendment, but in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kesavananda-bharati-v-state-of-kerala">Kesavananda Bharati v. State of Kerala</a> the Supreme Court held that certain features are so integral to the constitution that they can never be removed, a principle known as the Basic Structure doctrine. The Hindi translation was the first into an Indian language, made by the linguist Raghu Vira.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Constitution of India” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…joined on 15 May 1949, the last remaining state, Sirohi, was divided: on 26 January 1950, the day the <b>Constitution of India</b> came into force, most of Sirohi was merged into the union, but Abu and Delwara, with the hill station of Mount Abu, went to Bombay State.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-state-of-rajasthan">United State of Rajasthan · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Constitution of India</b>, drafted under Ambedkar’s chairmanship, made the country the Republic of India on 26 January 1950, with votes for all adults and untouchability abolished.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Constitution of India</b> came into force and Rajendra Prasad became the first President, replacing the British monarch as head of state.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-26">26 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Constitution of India</b> came into force on 26 January 1950, making India a sovereign republic within the Commonwealth and ending the British monarch's role as head of state.</blockquote><a href="/state/dominion-of-india">Dominion of India</a></li></ul></section>
